`GCAllocator` now leaves reclamation to the garbage collector

$(REF_ALTTEXT `GCAllocator`, GCAllocator, std, experimental, allocator, gc_allocator)
no longer provides `deallocate` or moving `reallocate`. Use
$(REF_ALTTEXT `GCHeapMallocator`, GCHeapMallocator, std, experimental, allocator, gc_allocator)
when individual release or moving reallocation of GC-scanned storage is needed.

Individual deallocation is optional in the static allocator protocol. Its
absence does not specify how storage is eventually reclaimed. Construction
helpers accept such allocators and destroy constructed array elements when
initialization fails, releasing storage individually only when supported.
`dispose` requires `deallocate`, as does the parent of `ScopedAllocator`.
`StatsCollector` now exposes `deallocate` only when its parent does.
The fixed dynamic allocator interfaces continue to return `false` when the
underlying allocator does not provide individual deallocation.

The default `theAllocator` and `processAllocator` wrap `GCAllocator`, so their
`deallocate` now returns `false` and `theAllocator.dispose` destroys the object
but no longer frees its storage; the collector reclaims it.

Wrappers forward `deallocate` only when their parent provides it. Over
`GCAllocator`, `AffixAllocator`, `Quantizer` and `StatsCollector` no longer
provide `deallocate`, and neither they nor `TypedAllocator` support `dispose`
any more; such calls no longer compile. `Segregator` provides `deallocate`
when either of its allocators does, and returns `false` for blocks from the
side that cannot deallocate; a `Segregator` with `GCAllocator` on both sides
has no `deallocate`. Use `GCHeapMallocator` as the parent to keep explicit
release.

`expandArray` and `shrinkArray` with `GCAllocator` no longer use `GC.realloc`.
Expansion tries `GCAllocator.expand` in place and otherwise allocates a new
block and copies, leaving the old block to the collector. Shrinking with an
allocator that provides neither `deallocate` nor `reallocate` slices the array
in place instead of copying it to a smaller block.

Generic reallocation in `std.experimental.allocator.common` still supports
allocators without individual deallocation by allocating a replacement and
retaining the old storage for the allocator's lifetime policy to handle.
